  abstract = {Many scholars agree that political discussions in heterogeneous group settings can decrease polarization, while discussions in homogeneous settings might increase polarization. The strongest causal evidence for these patterns comes from forced-exposure experiments in which participants are randomly assigned to a group discussion context. However, Americans have clear preferences for avoiding political discussions, especially with those who disagree with them. In this study, we incorporate self-selection into the standard political discussion experimental design. We use a Preference Incorporating Choice and Assignment (PICA) design to estimate a discussion setting’s effect conditional on choice to receive (or avoid) it. We find that regardless of the discussion setting one would choose if given the choice, heterogeneous discussion reduces polarization, but homogeneous discussion has no effect on polarization. While self-selection does not condition attitudinal effects of polarization, it has behavioral consequences. People who would choose a homogeneous setting, but experienced a heterogeneous discussion in the study, were less willing to engage in future discussions. Our findings have implications for scaling discussion-based interventions designed to reduce polarization.},
